BUGS
     You can't _c_l_o_s_e a file descriptor which you have _v_r_e_a_d from
     while there are still pages in the file which haven't been
     fetched by the system into your address space.  In no case
     can a file descriptor which had such pages at the point of a
     _v_f_o_r_k be closed during the _v_f_o_r_k.

     The system refuses to truncate a file to which any process
     has a pending _v_r_e_a_d.

     There is no primitive inverting _v_r_e_a_d to release the binding
     _v_r_e_a_d sets up so that the file may be closed.  This can be
     only be done, clumsily, by reading another (plain) file onto
     the buffer area, or pulling the break back with _b_r_e_a_k(2) to
     completely release the pages.

     This call is peculiar to this version of UNIX.  It will be
     superseded by more general virtual memory facilities in
     future versions of the system.




































